From 4d1ee7c3db842730692ecc2423f96647d873390f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 17:22:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/23] BananaDS perf: the windows' loops and the 3D layer's
 conversion vectorized; at 2x a windowed line keeps the raw 3D path
 (render.2d_win_neon)

ApplyWindow (a layer line masked by the window mask's bit, run once per drawn layer when a window is on) and
DrawBG_3D (the 3D line converted to a BG line: opaque where the alpha byte is nonzero, with the 3D flag) were
scalar 256-pixel loops, ~6 instructions a pixel. At 2x with a window on, the 3D layer could not take the raw path
(the compositor's own conversion needs no window), so each of a line's four quarters went through DrawBG_3D and
ApplyWindow: eight scalar passes a line, ~12k instructions, more than the quarters' merge itself (2.2k). HUD windows
over a 3D scene are the common game case; no test ROM had windows and 3D together (ds2dtest: windows, no 3D;
membench, geomtest, dsstress: 3D, no windows), which is why the profiles never showed it.

render.2d_win_neon (default on, render.2d_fast builds): ApplyWindow sixteen pixels a step (the mask bytes tested
against the bit and widened to lane masks by zips), DrawBG_3D four a step, and at 2x with render.2d_quarters a
windowed line keeps Raw3D: MergeQuarter takes the window mask and ANDs it into the 3D pixel's opacity, exactly
DrawBG_3D's line after ApplyWindow (the flag OR and the alpha test are the same ops). The MergeQuarter template
gains a `win` parameter (raw only; 12 more instantiations); DrawScanlineFrom skips the ApplyWindow of a raw 3D line.
Off: the scalar loops and the converted, masked quarter lines as before.

Exact: evidence/render/win_test.cpp (device compiler, qemu): 5000 random cases, ApplyWindow for every layer bit
(25000) 0 mismatches, DrawBG_3D 0, the merge with the window against DrawBG_3D + ApplyWindow + the merge without
(12 variants, 60000) 0. Measured on a membench variant with WIN0 over the 3D scene (evidence/render, membench-win).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
